---
import ProjectPost from "../../../components/ProjectPost.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import cover from "./20250125-1.png";

export const meta = {
    title: "Realistic .NET integration tests with xunit and testcontainers",
    date: new Date(2025, 0, 25),
    description: "Spinning up real Postgres and Redis instead of mocking them",
    tech: [".NET", "Postgres"],
    role: ["Engineering"],
    image: cover,
};
---

<ProjectPost meta={meta}>
    <p>
        Unit tests leave much to be desired when writing tests for a server
        linked to a database. I wanted an easy way to test a server to improve
        maintainability and the velocity of shipping. After evaluation of
        available options, I chose to write tests using <a
            href="https://xunit.net/docs/getting-started/v3/getting-started"
            >xunit.v3</a
        > and spinning up dependencies like Postgres and Redis using <a
            href="https://dotnet.testcontainers.org/">testcontainers</a
        >.
    </p><h2>Test runner; run the tests</h2><p>
        The <a href="https://xunit.net/docs/shared-context#assembly-fixture"
            >AssemblyFixture</a
        > in xunit.v3 has been a life-saver for having singletons across the test
        runner. The following is the standard setup for a <code
            >WebApiFixture</code
        > modified to our requirements.
    </p><p>
        In this project, both <code>Collections</code> and <code>TestCases</code
        > are run in alphabetical order, sequentially. Parallel execution of tests
        is turned off since that model of running tests is not compatible with having
        a single database instance.
    </p><h2>Abstracting away basic CRUD tests</h2><p>
        With the generic reflection-based <code>Faker</code>, CRUD operations
        can be done easily without having to define any of the parameters. This
        also serves as a good test for how well the <code>[Attributes]</code> handle
        the inputs since the Faker can add some <em>flakiness</em> to the tests.
    </p><p>
        With how the <code>CrudIntegrationTest</code> abstract class is written, tests
        that inherit the class will always run a series of CRUD actions on the endpoint
        provided in the constructor. This makes authoring a basic set of tests very
        easy for endpoints that behave similarly, which was most of the code-base
        in this case.
    </p><p>
        Furthermore, having a running instance of the production server,
        database, and Faker helped me test locale support, new
        database versions, and SQL query stability across ORM upgrades.
    </p><p>
        <Image
            src={cover}
            alt="An open IDE window shows sequential tests in Alphabetical order passing."
            class="w-full"
        />
    </p><h2>Next steps</h2><ol>
        <li>
            While this worked well for a monolithic system, I'm not sure how
            well this approach works for a microservice architecture.
        </li><li>
            Intuitively, I feel like there has to be a point where the
            testcontainers set-up becomes impractical/limited compared to a
            conventional Docker Compose based runner.
        </li>
    </ol>
</ProjectPost>
